men.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Vitamin D formulations interact with antiepileptic and phosphorus-containing drugs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Vitamin D滴剂 (vitamin D drops) and its capsule formulation exhibit clinically significant drug interactions. Structured data indicate that antiepileptic drugs such as phenytoin can reduce the efficacy of vitamin D, likely by accelerating its metabolism [Data: Relationships (48155)]. Separately, co-administration of vitamin D with phosphorus-containing medications may lead to hyperphosphatemia, while concurrent use with high-dose calcium or certain diuretics raises the risk of hypercalcemia [Data: Relationships (48129, 3902)]. These interactions highlight the need for careful management of mineral and vitamin supplementation in patients on polypharmacy regimens, particularly those involving anticonvulsants or electrolyte-modifying agents.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Primidone requires individualized dosing due to variable blood concentrations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Therapeutic use of primidone is complicated by significant inter-individual variability in blood drug concentrations. Clinical guidance notes that this variability necessitates personalized dosing strategies to ensure efficacy and avoid toxicity [Data: Relationships (2097)]. This pharmacokinetic characteristic increases the complexity of managing patients on primidone, especially in populations with comorbidities or those taking interacting medications. Monitoring primidone blood levels is thus a critical component of safe clinical practice, reinforcing the drug’s position as a high-attention therapeutic agent within this community.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Cardiac electrophysiological effects of primidone suggest antiarrhythmic potential\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Evidence suggests that primidone may exert beneficial effects on severe arrhythmias, as demonstrated by a case where its use—combined with propranolol—led to control of syncope and elimination of serious arrhythmias. This outcome was accompanied by measurable improvements in electrocardiographic parameters, including shortened corrected QT interval and reduced T-U wave amplitude [Data: Relationships (2096, 2098)]. The proposed mechanism involves modulation of transmembrane ion fluxes (Na⁺ and K⁺) during myocardial repolarization. While primarily an anticonvulsant, this off-target cardiac effect positions primidone as a compound with potential secondary cardiovascular implications, warranting further investigation and cautious use in patients with underlying heart conditions.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Diagnostic interference by primidone necessitates protocol adjustments\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Primidone is documented to interfere with the phentolamine test, a diagnostic procedure used in evaluating certain endocrine disorders such as pheochromocytoma. The drug can produce false-positive results, which may lead to misdiagnosis if not accounted for [Data: Relationships (2101)]. Clinical guidelines recommend discontinuing primidone for at least 24 hours—and ideally 48 to 72 hours—prior to conducting the test. This requirement illustrates how a single medication can disrupt standard diagnostic workflows, emphasizing the need for interdisciplinary communication between neurologists, endocrinologists, and laboratory personnel when managing patients on long-term antiepileptic therapy.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Vitamin D capsule formulation specifically linked to hypercalcemia risk\",\n            \"explanation\": \"While both liquid and capsule forms of vitamin D are present in the community, only the capsule variant (维生素D滴剂胶囊型) is explicitly associated with hypercalcemia in structured records. This risk is heightened when co-administered with high-dose calcium supplements or certain antihypertensive diure